    "opener": {
      "id": "opener",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A profile is a selectable convention that says how a corpus is laid out, which standards an author is shown, and which templates "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docs_create",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " offers. The existing software convention becomes one profile. The "
        },
        {
          "insert": "document model",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/lascari-ai/knowledge-system/docs/40-document-model"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and the writing guidance stay shared across all profiles. Repo docs keep the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "software",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " profile, and knowledge Docs in Places and Topics use the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "knowledge",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " profile."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "status": {
      "id": "status",
      "type": "callout",
      "props": {
        "kind": "note"
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "This draft spec was consolidated on 2026-10-02 from the Second Brain drafts of 2026-09-19. Its knowledge profile follows draft 5 of the knowledge system, and nothing here is implemented."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "existing-h": {
      "id": "existing-h",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Existing Behavior"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "existing-p1": {
      "id": "existing-p1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The software convention has four normative layers: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "00-foundation",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "10-system-design",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "20-agents",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "30-implementation",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". Extension tiers from "
        },
        {
          "insert": "40-",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " through "
        },
        {
          "insert": "98-",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " hold non-normative procedure, while "
        },
        {
          "insert": "99-appendix",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " holds appendix and meta material. Every folder uses a two-digit prefix, normally with gaps of ten, so filesystem order is reading order. The docs-system corpus is itself a software-profile corpus, and its "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Specs",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "90-specs"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " tier holds drafts such as this spec."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "existing-p2": {
      "id": "existing-p2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The implementation layer mirrors the source tree and links design constraints to the code that realizes them. Code linking connects claims to source locations, while in-code docs carry file contracts and function contracts below the three documentation levels. Standards and design pages commonly use the page shape Structure, The Rule, and Why."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "existing-p3": {
      "id": "existing-p3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Enforcement currently couples every corpus to this convention. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docs_begin",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " loads standards only from the docs-system corpus and has no per-project override. The docs-system skill states the reading order as Foundation, System Design, Agents, then Implementation. The "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docs audit",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " CLI requires "
        },
        {
          "insert": "NN-",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " folder names, although "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docs_check",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " does not run that audit. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "docs_create",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " yields a blank page, so templates and interview scripts exist only as prose in standards and the framework skill."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "existing-p4": {
      "id": "existing-p4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Two parts already apply to any subject. The style guide defines matter-of-fact voice, the join test, Title Case headings, useful openings, and unslop. Document Purpose distinguishes tutorial, how-to, reference, and explanation without assuming software."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "network-h": {
      "id": "network-h",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "What Network Setup Showed"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "network-p": {
      "id": "network-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Network Setup",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/knowledge/network-setup/docs"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " migration on 2026-09-18 exposed the cost of treating one layout as universal. The corpus's real deliverable was the runbook, but the runbook sat in a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "40-",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " extension tier that the standards call non-normative. Agents and Implementation were filler: one named generic roles, and the other documented build scripts instead of the network. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "99-appendix",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " became Sources and Archive, while the Foundation page hand-wrote a reading order because the corpus could not declare one."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "network-p2": {
      "id": "network-p2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "By 2026-09-30 Ford had removed Agents and Implementation and reorganized "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Network Setup",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/knowledge/network-setup/docs"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " into setup guides such as "
        },
        {
          "insert": "firewall setup",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/knowledge/network-setup/docs/20-firewall-setup"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "device directory",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/knowledge/network-setup/docs/50-devices"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and dated "
        },
        {
          "insert": "current-state pages",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "source",
              "path": "/Users/Ford/workspace/codebases/knowledge/network-setup/docs/99-appendix"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". That reorganization is a runbook-profile layout built by hand inside the software profile, which supports making profiles selectable."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
